{
 "lead": "Every bulk forming load is a mean flow stress times a contact area, raised by a friction and shape factor. The mean flow stress σ̄ is the average stress that keeps the metal yielding through the pass. The contact area comes from the process geometry. Load estimation tells you whether a mill, press or die can take the force and whether the motor can supply the power. Only a few universities teach this chapter in detail.",
 "prereq": "You need: <a href=\"#plastic-deformation-yield-criteria-hot-and-cold-working\">2.1</a> for flow stress, and <a href=\"#rolling-principle-rolling-mills-and-defects\">2.2</a> to <a href=\"#wire-and-tube-drawing\">2.5</a> for the contact geometry of each process.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Stress times contact area",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Bulk forming load follows one pattern: load = mean flow stress × contact area × correction. The mean flow stress σ̄ (MPa; use Pa in the formulas) is the average stress needed to keep the metal yielding over the strain of that pass; use it, not the initial yield stress, because strain and stress vary through the zone. Contact area A (m²) is the area where tool touches work, not the whole billet. The correction K (dimensionless) covers friction and shape. For extrusion the result is written as a pressure: p = σ̄(a + b ln R). R = A₀/A_f is the extrusion ratio; A₀ is billet area and A_f is product area; a and b are constants set by die angle and friction. Ideal frictionless cold extrusion has a = 0 and b = 1, so p = σ̄ ln R. A friction estimate can use b ≈ 1.5, which lifts pressure at every R. This is the only load idea in this chapter."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Rolling, forging and drawing loads",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Rolling uses the projected contact area under one roll, A = wL. Use σ̄ in Pa and lengths in m. F = σ̄ w L. w is strip width (m); L = √(RΔh) is the projected contact length (m); R is roll radius (m); Δh = h₀ − h₁ is the draft (m), with h₀ inlet and h₁ exit thickness. Torque per roll T = F L/2 (N·m). Total power P = 2Tω for two rolls, with ω = 2πN/60 rad/s and N mill speed in rev/min."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Forging: F = σ̄ A K_f. A is plan area. For a disc of radius r and height h, K_f ≈ 1 + 2μr/(3h); μ is friction coefficient. Extrusion force is p times ram area, with p = σ̄(a + b ln R). Drawing stress σ_d = σ̄ ln(A₀/A_f) × φ; φ > 1 is a friction factor, and σ_d must stay below yield or the wire breaks. Ideal work per unit volume w_ideal = σ̄ ln(A₀/A_f) for drawing or extrusion. Efficiency η = w_ideal/w_actual. Choose press tonnage above peak load with a margin. Energy per part = average force × stroke."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
